Now I see a critical issue! `Term::stdout().as_raw_fd()` returns `libc::STDOUT_FILENO` (fd 1).

In bivvy's `RawModeGuard::enter(term)`:
```rust
fn enter(term: &Term) -> std::io::Result<Self> {
    use std::os::unix::io::AsRawFd;
    let fd = term.as_raw_fd();  // This is STDOUT (fd 1)!

    unsafe {
        libc::tcgetattr(fd, ...);  // tcgetattr on fd 1 (stdout)
        libc::cfmakeraw(&mut raw);
        libc::tcsetattr(fd, libc::TCSADRAIN, &raw);  // raw mode on fd 1
    }
}
```

But `console`'s `read_single_key()` is actually checking stdin (fd 0) and applying raw mode there, while bivvy is applying it to stdout (fd 1). So the two libraries are operating on different file descriptors entirely—bivvy puts stdout in raw mode while console expects stdin to be in raw mode, which explains why the input handling breaks. So `console`'s `read_single_key` effectively leaves the terminal in whatever state `RawModeGuard` set it. But there's a subtle issue when `RawModeGuard` isn't active—if the user hits Ctrl+D before `console` switches to raw mode, the kernel's line discipline processes it as EOF on an empty buffer, and that EOF condition persists in the read queue when `read_single_key` is later called.

Once we switch from canonical to non-canonical mode though, the canonical input buffer gets discarded and any pending EOF from a previous Ctrl+D shouldn't carry over to non-canonical reads. The real question is whether we're dealing with an actual EOF from the PTY master closing, which would cause `read()` to return 0 regardless of the mode we're in.

